thank you dear cecilia bonfertal and  dear friends of digital europe ladies  and gentlemen  since our virtual meeting  approximately a year ago at masters of  digital  i have embarked on a journey across our  digital europe  i met for example companies in  luxembourg that are building  an ultra secure satellite communication  system  thanks to quantum technology  i listen to students in ireland who are  using artificial intelligence  to improve animal health in farms  and i spoke to startupers in slovakia  who are bringing new technologies to the  market  from nanosensors  to rna sequencings for example  so each of them had an incredible story  to tell  stories of entrepreneurship  stories of innovation  innovation at the frontiers of human  knowledge  stories that you all know very well  because these are your stories  too and all of these encounters have  given me so much confidence  about europe's digital future  and one year ago  i gave you a promise  i promised we would strive to make the  2020s  europe's digital decade  in two ways with unprecedented  investment  and with a modern set of rules for  europe's digital sector  now  one year on  we are delivering with both  funds and legislation  if you look at our recovery plan next  generation eu  it is investing in projects like those  i've just mentioned  next generation eu  has over  125 billion euros  in digital investment  so massive resources  that have already started to revitalize  and reshape our economy  i think this is our marshall plan for  digital europe  and besides investment we have started  to write a new rulebook for europe's  digital decade  first there is the digital markets act  and the digital services act  in those two acts we lay out the basic  principles  for all digital companies in europe  we kind of create a level playing field  for all businesses  and clear rights for all users  secondly  there is the artificial intelligence act  with this act we have created a guard  rail  for one of the most crucial technologies  of our age  of course we want companies and  programmers to keep advancing and  innovating and we're expanding our  investment in ai  but as we do this  we must protect people and their rights  with clear rules on the high risk  applications of ai  and let me mention in this regard  the winner of last year's future unicorn  award  by digital europe  is a hungarian company called encompass  they have developed a software  to support oncologists  in choosing the right therapy for  patients  and see thanks to artificial  intelligence  the software can forecast each patient's  response to thousands of targeted  therapies against cancer  so this is simply amazing  this could be the next scientific  revolution  and i really believe ai must be a force  for good  for greater human welfare  and the ai act can truly channel the  power of artificial intelligence  in the right direction  in the coming weeks and months we will  add more chapters to the new rulebook  for our digital economy  for example later this month  we will present our data act  it will give businesses and individuals  more control over the data they produce  while using a connected device  and more control over how their data are  processed  and later this year we will come with  our cyber resilience act  which will establish common security  standards for all connected devices in  europe  so the digital transition needs clear  rules  people need to know  that they can trust the technology in  their hands  business need predictability to plan  their investment  and this is exactly why we have come up  with the most ambitious agenda  for digital reforms and investment  in our union's history  ladies and gentlemen  next week  we will present a crucial piece of this  agenda  and this is europe's first ever chips  act  and i would like to use this opportunity  to give you an insight  on our forthcoming proposal  semiconductors have become front page  news in these months  when the global chip shortage has slowed  down the global recovery  our factories closed in east asia  and entire production lines here in  europe came to a standstill  so we all realized what europe still  lacks  when it comes to one of the most crucial  assets for the digital transition  because we all know there is no digital  without chips  but allow me for a moment to reverse our  point of view  instead of focusing on what we do not  have  let me focus on europe's strength in the  field of semiconductors  europe is the world center  for semiconductor research  if today's advanced chips pack tens of  billions of transistors in each square  centimeter of silicon  that's thanks to european technology  and some of the essential machines  to produce the most advanced ships in  the world  are made in only one place in the world  right here  in europe  and we are strong in specific sectors  such as chips for cars or for industry  4.0  so they are crucial niche  where europe is a global leader  but i want us to become a strong player  all across the chip's value chain  and where others see deficiencies  i see potential  and this makes me confident that the  goal we have set for the european ships  act  is ambitious  but it is achievable  by 2030  20 of the world's microchips production  should be here in europe  that's twice as much as today  in a market that is set to double in the  next decade so it means quadrupling  today's european production  the european ships act will back this  ambition with considerable investment  it will enable more than 12 billion  euros in additional public and private  investment by 2030  and this will come on top of more than  30 billion euros of public investments  already foreseen  now backed by next generation eu horizon  europe  as well as national budgets  so these funds will be more than matched  by private investments  and to put numbers in perspective  european companies in the semiconductors  field are now investing roughly 6  billion euros per year  so the european ships act can be a game  changer  and our work will focus in particular on  five areas  first there is research  we will invest in a field where we are  already punching above our weight  focusing for instance on transistors  below three nanometers  and on disruptive technologies for  artificial intelligence  when it comes to research and innovation  europe can basically go from strength to  strength  second  from the lab to the fab  we must translate our excellent research  into industrial innovation  we will invest in ships design and in  pilot lines for prototypes  thus bridging the gap between the  laboratory  and the actual manufacturing of the most  innovative chips  my third point  is on production capacity  europe needs new advanced production  facilities  for instance europe relies entirely on  foreign producers  for leading edge ships  and these facilities come with huge  upfront costs therefore  public support can be necessary to  attract private investment  and for this reason  we are adapting our state aid rules  under a set of strict conditions but  this will now allow for the very first  time  public support  for european first-of-a-kind production  facilities which of course benefit all  of  europe so my fourth point is that this  is not just about mega fabs  and therefore the european ships act  will also support smaller innovative  companies  it will help them in accessing advanced  skills  or for example have industrial partners  and access to equity finance and finally  finally  my fifth point is on supply chains  it should be clear that no country  and even no continent  can be entirely self-sufficient  so europe will always work to keep  global markets open and connected  it's in the world's interest and it's in  our own interest  but we need to tackle the bottlenecks  that slow down our growth  a situation where entire industrial  sectors  rely on only one or two producers in  east asia is simply not sustainable  and certainly not in today's world  so to change this  we will work on semiconductor  partnerships with like-minded partners  from the united states to japan for  example  and with that we can create more  balanced interdependencies  we can avoid single points of failure  and we can build supply chains  that we can truly trust  ladies and gentlemen dear friends  last fall i visited asml in antophan  one of our european champions for  semiconductors  machines designed and built here are  crucial to produce ships in all corners  of the world indeed from singapore to  the united states  and it's not our only champion  infineon  nxp  st microelectronics just to name a few  europe is also becoming more attractive  for foreign giants like intel  and europe keeps leading the world on  innovation  think of our research centers  for instance imac in belgium  they are spearheading  the efforts to create a new generation  of energy efficient  and climate-friendly chips  and this is why i believe we can achieve  the goals we have set with the european  ships act and europe's digital decade  we are putting out the investment  and the strategy  but the key to our success  lies in europe's innovators  world-class researchers  in the people  who have made our continent prosper  through the decades  europe is the continent where all the  industrial revolutions have started  and europe can be the home of the next  industrial revolution too  so let's join forces  to make it happen  and ladies and gentlemen dear friends  last  but not least  through the master of digital awards  you honor people and companies  who help drive europe's digital  transformation  and thus  i am truly honored  to be among the winners of this year's  award and let me show it  the wonderful award here it is  thank you very much for that i am really  honored  i take it as a recognition of the  commission's engagement to deliver the  digital decade  and of course congratulations  to the other winners of the master of  digital awards  and thank you very much for your  attention
 

